here are several examples of using the netstat command to solve various problems:

To list all open ports:

netstat -an
This will list all open ports on your computer, including the local and remote addresses and the state of the connection.

To list all listening ports:

netstat -l
This will list all ports that are listening for connections, but not yet connected.

To find a specific port that is in use:

netstat -an | findstr :<port number>

For example, to find the process that is using port 80, you would use the following command:

netstat -an | findstr :80

To troubleshoot a network connection problem:
netstat -an | findstr <remote host>
For example, to troubleshoot a connection to the host www.google.com, you would use the following command:

netstat -an | findstr www.google.com

To monitor network traffic:

netstat -an -b
This will list all open ports and the processes that are using them. It will also show the network traffic that is being sent and received on each port.

These are just a few examples of how the netstat command can be used to troubleshoot network problems. The specific command that you will need to use will depend on the nature of the problem you are troubleshooting.

Here are some additional command line switches that you may find useful:

-a - Display all connections, including listening ports.
-b - Display the process ID (PID) of the process that is using each port.
-n - Display addresses and ports numerically, instead of using names.
-o - Display the state of each connection.
-r - Display the routing table.


